Description: X11: the window scale may be fractional (Stained Glass OS)
 GDK's X11 backend scales windows by a whole number (GDK_SCALE, the
 XSETTINGS Gdk/WindowScalingFactor): at a display scale of 175% GTK drew at
 200%. With the XSETTINGS manager's Gdk/SgFractionalScale (the scale in
 1024ths, 1792 at 175%) a window is that many pixels per logical one: its X
 window is the logical size times the scale, cairo draws into it at the
 scale (crisp text and shapes; images made at the next whole step and drawn
 smaller), events are divided by it -- core and XInput2 events alike (XI2's,
 truncated to 1, put every click 1.75 times too far from the window's corner:
 Inkscape's buttons did nothing on a Surface, 2026-10-07) -- and the text's
 DPI is Xft/DPI over the scale. gdk_window_get_scale_factor() gives the
 next whole step. Programs
 that draw into their X windows themselves by GDK's whole-number factor
 (Firefox, Thunderbird, Emacs, LibreOffice; GDK_SG_FRACTIONAL=0 for any
 other) are told scale 1 and the whole DPI, and scale themselves. Programs
 that paint their own canvas from a store at the whole-number factor
 (Inkscape, whose canvas was not repainted when it scrolled;
 GDK_SG_FRACTIONAL=whole for any other) keep whole steps, as Debian's GTK
 does: 200% at 175%. OpenGL
 painting is read back through cairo at a fractional scale. Without
 Gdk/SgFractionalScale, or with GDK_SCALE set, GTK behaves as before; at a
 whole-number scale every size is computed as before.
Author: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2026-10-05
